---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro'
import { site } from '@/data/site'
---

<Base
  title="Hero Notch privacy — local by default, zero telemetry"
  description="Complete breakdown of what Hero Notch stores on your Mac or Windows PC, how local Markdown notes, webcam posture, Spotify PKCE, Google Calendar, AI sessions, and Wi-Fi phone transfer work."
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Privacy Architecture</span>
      <h1>Local by default.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Notch has no telemetry server, no user analytics SDK, and no cloud account requirement. Your
        Markdown notes, posture camera frames, AI session history, and local Wi-Fi transfers stay on
        your hardware.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Every file Notch stores on your PC</h2>
      <p>
        All application state lives inside your Windows user profile under
        <code>%APPDATA%\notch</code> and inside the local notes folder you choose:
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Local Path</th>
            <th>What It Stores</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\notch-state.json</code></td>
            <td>
              Workspace preferences, planner tasks, focus timer intervals, daily hydration pours,
              posture calibration baseline &amp; daily score totals, and your selected notes vault
              path.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\spotify-auth.json</code></td>
            <td>
              Optional Spotify Web API OAuth PKCE access and refresh tokens (loopback redirect on
              <code>127.0.0.1:43821</code>). No client secret is stored.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\calendar-session.enc</code></td>
            <td>
              Optional Google Calendar session token encrypted on disk using Windows DPAPI
              (Electron <code>safeStorage</code>) after loopback sign-in on
              <code>127.0.0.1:43822</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\agent-token.json</code></td>
            <td>
              Random 32-byte local bearer token authenticating localhost agent status hooks on
              <code>127.0.0.1:7432</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>%APPDATA%\notch\notch.log</code></td>
            <td>Local startup, renderer recovery, and diagnostic log lines. Never uploaded.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Your Markdown / Obsidian folder</td>
            <td>
              Plain <code>.md</code> and <code>.txt</code> files read and edited directly in place.
              Paths are validated to stay inside your selected folder, and deletions move files to
              the Windows Recycle Bin.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Local Markdown &amp; Obsidian notes vault</h2>
      <p>
        Notch reads and writes your Markdown files directly through the local filesystem. It
        automatically ignores <code>.obsidian</code>, <code>.git</code>, and <code>.trash</code>
        directories and never uploads, indexes, or mirrors your notes to any external server.
      </p>

      <h2>On-device webcam posture coach</h2>
      <p>
        The posture coach is <strong>off by default</strong>. When you turn it on, webcam frames are
        evaluated in memory inside the local renderer process using MediaPipe pose landmarks,
        converted into a 0–100 alignment score, and discarded immediately. No images or video
        streams are ever written to disk or transmitted over the network. On first activation, the
        pose model weights and WASM runtime are downloaded once from
        <code>cdn.jsdelivr.net</code> and <code>storage.googleapis.com</code> and cached locally by
        Chromium. The hardware camera LED stays lit whenever the camera is active.
      </p>

      <h2>Spotify &amp; Windows system media</h2>
      <p>
        Basic playback control reads the native Windows Global System Media Transport Controls
        (GSMTC) session and requires no account or network request. The optional full Spotify Web
        API connection uses OAuth 2.0 PKCE over a temporary localhost callback and talks directly to
        Spotify’s API. Clicking <strong>Disconnect</strong> deletes your local token file.
      </p>

      <h2>Google Calendar &amp; Day Planner</h2>
      <p>
        Local planner tasks and under-notch reminders run entirely offline from
        <code>notch-state.json</code>. If you optionally connect Google Calendar, OAuth sign-in uses
        a loopback listener on <code>127.0.0.1:43822</code> and encrypts your session token locally
        with Windows DPAPI (<code>safeStorage</code>). You can disconnect and purge the encrypted
        session file at any time from Settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Multi-agent coding sessions (Codex, OpenCode, Anti Gravity &amp; Claude Code)</h2>
      <p>
        The Sessions board opens the local SQLite and JSONL state files already written by your
        installed coding tools (such as <code>~/.codex/</code>,
        <code>~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db</code>, and
        <code>~/.gemini/antigravity/conversation_summaries.db</code>) in <strong>read-only</strong>
        mode. When you send a reply from the notch composer, Notch invokes that tool’s own local CLI
        or local language-server interface on your machine; your prompt goes directly to that
        provider under their respective privacy policy, never through a Notch server.
      </p>

      <h2>Local Wi-Fi phone QR transfer</h2>
      <p>
        When you open the Phone Transfer modal, Notch starts a temporary HTTP server bound strictly
        to your local LAN interface with a random one-time cryptographic token in the QR URL. Files
        (up to 16 MB) and text snippets travel directly between your phone’s browser and your PC
        over your local Wi-Fi router — zero cloud relays, zero external servers. Closing the modal
        shuts down the local listener immediately.
      </p>

      <h2>Focus mode desktop backdrop</h2>
      <p>
        When Focus Mode is enabled, Notch captures a temporary local snapshot of your primary
        display while collapsed so it can render a blurred backdrop behind the open notch. That
        bitmap lives only on your machine and is never transmitted anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>This website &amp; license checkout</h2>
      <p>
        This website uses cookieless, privacy-preserving pageview metrics with no cross-site
        tracking or advertising pixels. Licensed release installers are delivered directly to paying
        customers after purchase. When you buy a license, payment processing is handled by our
        checkout provider — we never see or store your credit card number, and we retain only your
        purchase email address to deliver your license receipt and updates.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting everything</h2>
      <p>
        Uninstall Notch from Windows Settings (or delete the portable executable), then delete the
        <code>%APPDATA%\notch</code> directory. Your local Markdown notes folder remains untouched on
        your drive. Questions? Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
